Why This Architecture Is Better

This architecture preserves the separation of concerns established throughout the ingestion pipeline.

Operational Knowledge

↓

Runbooks

Reusable Procedures

↓

Playbooks

Diagnostic Reasoning

↓

Runtime

Interactive Troubleshooting

If Stage 8 embeds runbooks directly into playbooks, this separation is lost.

Runbooks become duplicated.

Playbooks become bloated.

Maintenance becomes significantly harder because updating a runbook would require updating every playbook that contains it.

Instead, the playbook should simply orchestrate reusable procedures.

Cursor Implementation Rules

Stage 8 should follow these rules without exception:

Preserve every Stage 5 node exactly. Never remove, merge, or rewrite diagnostic reasoning.
Treat Stage 5 as the authoritative source for workflow structure and branching.
Separate diagnostic reasoning ("what are we trying to determine?") from procedure execution ("how do we collect this evidence?").
Resolve evidence requirements into reusable procedure references rather than embedding full runbooks.
Keep healthy, unhealthy, and inconclusive observations visible and unchanged.
Present procedures as references with IDs, titles, purpose, and required role—not expanded step-by-step instructions.
Surface catalog gaps as explicit missing capabilities instead of attempting poor matches.
Preserve source-backed explanations and confidence so every diagnostic decision remains traceable.
Optimize for readability. A support engineer should understand the workflow by reading the playbook alone; detailed runbooks should only be opened when needed.
The final Stage 8 artifact should feel like the original Stage 5 playbook with reusable procedures attached—not a completely different document.

This approach keeps the playbook as the diagnostic brain of the system while allowing the runbook library to evolve independently as reusable operational documentation. It also aligns much better with your long-term architecture of reusable runbooks, workflow orchestration, and future knowledge graph relationships, instead of tightly coupling everything into one monolithic artifact.